{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>How many are the days of the years of your life?</b> Because it had been told to Pharaoh that when Jacob arrived the Nile rose toward him, and Pharaoh rejoiced over this exceedingly and thought that for as long as Jacob lived the Nile would always rise toward him and water the face of the land. But when he saw Jacob — and behold, he was gaunt and emaciated and thin of flesh, as is the way of the elderly — Pharaoh thought that surely he was advanced in old age and would die this very day or tomorrow, and if so, what did the Egyptians have to rejoice about, for upon his death the Nile would return to its place. Therefore he asked him, How many are the days of your life? And he answered him, ״יְמֵי שְׁנֵי מְגוּרַי מְעַט וְרָעִים״ (Genesis 47:9). He used the expression ״מְגוּרַי״ to say that it was not on account of the abundance of years that he was so gaunt and thin, but rather on account of fear and dread from every side, ״כִּי אֵת אֲשֶׁר יָגֹרְתִּי יָבֹא לִי״ (Job 3:25). And how many troubles were packed in my bundles against me all the span of a hundred and thirty years. Few and evil have been the days of my life, and they have not reached the years of my fathers. And I still have hope to reach the age of my father, who lived a hundred and eighty years; even though they too had dread from every side, nevertheless they lived long. Therefore he used the expression ״מְגוּרֵיהֶם״ also with regard to his fathers."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {0 "In the final clause, the text shifts from singular מְגוּרַי to plural מְגוּרֵיהֶם ('their sojournings'); the Kli Yakar is explaining why Jacob used this term also for his fathers in Genesis 47:9."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:33:48.169812699Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}